# Nobody knows what the stores go to. They name fields of the layouts that may hide behind such a value and of whose objects a written type declares the name.
# No layout with objects is written as WideLinks: only a cast leads to it, so it speaks of the objects of the nearest type above it that some layout is written as. Nothing is written of `stranger`, and a method lies above the objects.
# What some written type requires holds a value in whoever has it. What is optional everywhere is often stored undefined only to give the objects one shape, and takes no storage for that. A cell of 24 bytes takes 32: the word that is left over takes one of the two names.
